# {{projectName}}

The ONLYOFFICE DocSpace Webhooks SDK for Python is a library that provides tools for receiving webhook deliveries from DocSpace. It decodes each delivery into ready-to-use models.

It is the inbound counterpart to the DocSpace API SDK: that library calls DocSpace, this one handles what DocSpace sends you. It makes no requests of its own.

For more information, please visit [https://helpdesk.onlyoffice.com/hc/en-us](https://helpdesk.onlyoffice.com/hc/en-us)

## Requirements

Python 3.11+

## Installation

```
pip install {{projectName}}
```

## Usage

A receiver does two things, in this order: verify the signature, then decode.

Verify against the **raw request body**. A body that has been parsed and re-serialized is a different byte sequence and will never match, so hash the bytes that arrived before calling `json.loads`.

Answer as soon as the signature checks out and do the real work afterwards: the server waits 30 seconds for a response. A failed delivery is retried after about 1 min, 5 min, 30 min, 2 h, 5 h and 10 h (each ±10%) — seven attempts over roughly 18 hours by default — after which it is abandoned; a subscription that goes three days without a single successful delivery is switched off.

## Getting Started

```python
import hashlib
import hmac
import json

from flask import Flask, request

from {{packageName}}.models.file_entry_payload import FileEntryPayload
from {{packageName}}.models.user_payload import UserPayload
from {{packageName}}.models.webhook_event_info import WebhookEventInfo

app = Flask(__name__)

SECRET = "YOUR_SUBSCRIPTION_SECRET_KEY"


def verify(body, signature):
    """Constant-time, case-insensitive check over the raw body.

    DocSpace emits UPPERCASE hexadecimal where some other services emit
    lowercase, so the comparison has to fold case.
    """
    if not signature:
        return False
    digest = hmac.new(SECRET.encode(), body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
    return hmac.compare_digest("sha256=" + digest.lower(), signature.strip().lower())


@app.post("/webhook")
def webhook():
    body = request.get_data()

    if not verify(body, request.headers.get("x-docspace-signature-256")):
        return "", 401

    envelope = json.loads(body)
    event = WebhookEventInfo.from_dict(envelope["event"])
    trigger = event.trigger

    if trigger.startswith("user."):
        user = UserPayload.from_dict(envelope["payload"])
        app.logger.info("%s: %s <%s>", trigger, user.user_name, user.email)
    else:
        # Files, folders and rooms each have their own schema; they share the
        # fields of FileEntryPayload, which is enough for a generic log line.
        # Use FilePayload / FolderPayload / RoomPayload for the rest.
        entry = FileEntryPayload.from_dict(envelope["payload"])
        kind = "file" if entry.file_entry_type == 2 else "folder"
        app.logger.info("%s: %s %r", trigger, kind, entry.title)

    # Acknowledge now; anything slow belongs after this point.
    return "", 200
```

## Documentation for Verification

Every delivery carries `x-docspace-signature-256`, an HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body keyed with the subscription's secret, formatted as `sha256=` followed by uppercase hexadecimal. Compare it with `hmac.compare_digest` and case-insensitively, as above.

Two further headers, `x-docspace-event-id` and `x-docspace-event-timestamp`, repeat `event.id` and `event.createOn` so an already-seen delivery can be dropped without reading the body. They are **not** covered by the signature: reject on them freely, but never accept on them. The values inside the verified body are the authoritative ones. The signature covers the body only; it does not prove when the request was sent, and `event.createOn` stays the same on every automatic and manual retry, so deduplicate on `event.id` instead of rejecting a delivery for its age.

Deduplicate on `event.id`. It is stable across every resend of one delivery — the server's automatic retries and a manual retry from the delivery log alike — so keep the ids you have seen for longer than the ~18-hour retry window: a manual retry can bring the same event back days later.

## Documentation for Events

`event.trigger` names the event that caused the delivery, for example `file.created`. `GET api/2.0/settings/webhook/triggers` lists every event, the value to subscribe with, and whether your role may subscribe to it.

Files, folders, rooms, agents and forms all deliver the same payload shape; use `file_entry_type` (1 folder, 2 file) to tell them apart. A file's name arrives in `title`, and a field missing from the JSON means empty, `False` or zero — never "unknown".

New events are added over time. Treat an unfamiliar `event.trigger` as something to ignore rather than an error, so an upgrade cannot break your endpoint.

